\par }{\b\ul 4. Instructions
\par }{
\par When you start }{\i Compass }{you should see the screen divided i
nto two parts. On the left, your home city, and the current date and time are shown together with (except on the Revo) the times of sunrise & sunset. Also shown is the altitude and azimuth of the Sun, Moon and the three stars Betelgeuse, Vega and Sirius. 
On the right is the compass face. }{\i Compass }{will show the position of the sun and moon and 3 stars if they are above the horizon. 
\par 
\par }{\b To find North}{, simply line up the centre of the compass, the position on the screen and the position in the sky of one of the heavenly bodies. The compass will then be correctly oriented. 
\par 
\par The screen is updated automatically once per minute (you can force an update by pressing Enter).
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs22\ul\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {Location
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs22\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {
\par To see the compass for a location which is not your home city, press }{\b Menu|Settings|From}{ or }{\b Ctrl+f }{or tap on the city name on the screen and then select the relevant city from Psion\rquote s \'93World\'94 database.
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs22\ul\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {Astronomical info
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs22\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {
\par The altitude (i.e. how high above the horizon) and azimuth (i.e. compass direction) of each of the 5 heavenly bodies is shown at the left of the screen. \'93Not up\'94 means below the horizon.
\par 
\par The position displayed on the compass screen shows both the altitude and the azimuth. The outer circle represents the horizon (i.e. altitude=0) and the centre of the compass represents the sky directly overhead (i.e. altitude=90\'ba).
\par 
\par You can see how the sun, moon or a star progresses across the sky over the course of any day by pressing }{\b Menu|View|Fixed date}{ or }{\b Ctrl+x}{ or tap the \'93Fixed date\'94 toolbar button. A
 symbol is drawn for every hour of the day (every 2 hours on the Revo). Every second symbol is replaced with the time of day. 
\par 
\par To change the body shown, press }{\b Menu|View|Body}{ and select whichever you want. If you press }{\b Ctrl+b}{ or tap the \'93Body\'94 toolbar button, the display goes on to the next one in the list. The name of the body displayed is shown at the left.

\par 
\par To see this 24-hour progression for a different date, press }{\b Menu|View|Set fixed date}{ or }{\b Ctrl+s}{ or (except on the Revo, which has fewer buttons) tap the \'93Set date\'94 toolbar button.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\ul Daylight Saving Time (DST)
\par }{
\par Note that }{\i Compass}{ assumes that the current time is the time set on your Psion. It also calculates automatically the changeover dates for Daylight Saving Time (summer time) according to the "Zone" 
settings in the Psion "World" database. The rules used are:
\par 
\par }{\b Zone\tab \tab DST starts\tab \tab DST ends\tab 
\par }{Europe\tab \tab last Sun in March\tab last Sun in Oct \tab 
\par North\tab \tab first Sun in Apr\tab \tab last Sun in Oct\tab 
\par South\tab \tab last Sun in Oct\tab \tab last Sun in March
\par Const\tab \tab none\tab \tab \tab none\tab 
\par 
\par To override automatic Daylight Saving Time, press }{\b Menu|Settings|DST}{ or }{\b Shift+Ctrl+D}{.  Select either \'93On\'94 or \'93Off\'94 as appropriate.
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs22\ul\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {Bearing towards another city
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\ilvl12\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs22\lang2057\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp1033 {
\par Select a second city by pressing }{\b Menu|Settings|To}{ or }{\b Ctrl+t}{. The direction of the shortest route from the curren
t location to that city will be shown. This takes into account that the Earth is spherical and so for example the route from London to Honolulu is almost due North.
\par 
\par The approximate distance between the two cities is shown in miles. To see it in kilometres, press }{\b Menu|View|Distance units|Kilometres}{ or }{\b Ctrl+u.}{
\par 
\par You can see the route on the world map by pressing }{\b Menu|View|Map}{ or }{\b Ctrl+m }{or by tapping on the \'93Map\'94 toolbar button}{\b .}{
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }}